const CARDS4 = [
 C("rc-cf","Disease Differences","Cystic fibrosis care","Thick salty mucus: lungs, pancreas, sweat.","Greasy stools, failure to thrive, chronic cough, salty skin.","Airway clearance before meals; enzymes with every meal and snack; high-calorie diet with extra salt.","Giving enzymes only once a day; CPT right after meals.","Missing enzymes means fat and protein aren't absorbed.","When should pancreatic enzymes be given?",["With every meal and snack","Once at bedtime","Only when stools are greasy","2 hours after meals"],0),
 C("rc-pyloric","Common Examination Traps","Pediatric GI classic signs","Pyloric: projectile vomiting, hungry after. Intussusception: currant-jelly stool. Hirschsprung: no meconium. Wilms: don't palpate.","Olive mass, sausage mass, ribbon stools, abdominal mass.","Hydrate first; prepare for procedure; report a normal stool in intussusception.","Palpating a Wilms tumor; feeding before pyloromyotomy.","Each sign points to a specific structural problem.","Currant-jelly stools in a toddler suggest:",["Intussusception","Pyloric stenosis","Hirschsprung disease","Gastroenteritis"],0),
 C("rc-shunt","Emergency Findings","VP shunt and infant ICP","Bulging fontanel, rising head size, high-pitched cry, sunset eyes.","Older child: headache, vomiting, irritability, lethargy.","Neuro checks; notify promptly; position per order.","Pumping the shunt unless ordered; ignoring vomiting.","A blocked shunt lets CSF build up and pressure rise.","A sign of increased ICP in a 6-month-old is:",["Bulging fontanel","Sunken fontanel","Low-pitched cry","Decreased head circumference"],0),
 C("rc-ssri","Medication Safety","SSRIs and serotonin syndrome","2–6 weeks to work; suicide risk early; never with MAOIs.","Serotonin syndrome: fever, agitation, tremor, clonus, diarrhea.","Stop the drug, notify, cool, support.","Combining with St. John's wort, tramadol, triptans, MAOIs without guidance.","Excess serotonin overstimulates nerves and muscles.","Which finding suggests serotonin syndrome?",["Hyperthermia with muscle twitching","Bradycardia with constipation","Hair loss","Weight gain"],0),
 C("rc-anorexia","High-Yield Clinical Judgment","Eating disorder safety","Medical stability first: HR, BP, K+, rhythm.","HR < 40, K+ low, orthostasis; refeeding syndrome signs.","Supervise meals and 1–2 hours after; consistent weights; slow refeeding.","Power struggles over food; weighing in street clothes.","Starvation and purging cause fatal dysrhythmias.","Priority finding in a client with anorexia:",["HR 38 and K+ 2.8","Lanugo","Dry skin","Cold intolerance"],0),
 C("rc-rights","Top Nursing Priorities","Restraints and client rights","Least restrictive first; needs an order; never for convenience.","Check every 15 minutes (or continuous per policy); release every 2 hours.","Offer fluids, toileting, ROM; document behavior and alternatives tried.","Tying to side rails; PRN restraint orders.","Restraints can cause injury and death; rights stay with the client.","Before applying restraints, the nurse should:",["Try less restrictive measures","Tie them to the side rails","Get a PRN order","Restrain all four limbs"],0),
 C("rc-preterm","Maternal-Newborn Emergencies","Preterm labor","Stop and mature: tocolytics plus betamethasone.","Low backache, pelvic pressure, contractions every ≤ 10 minutes before 37 weeks.","Left side, hydrate, monitor, betamethasone ×2 24 hours apart, tocolytics per order.","Terbutaline with maternal HR > 120.","Each day in the uterus matters; steroids help lungs in 48 hours.","Betamethasone in preterm labor is given to:",["Speed fetal lung maturity","Stop contractions","Lower maternal BP","Prevent infection"],0),
 C("rc-o2","Important Nursing Procedures","Oxygen devices and ABGs","ROME; NC 1–6 L; Venturi = exact %; NRB 10–15 L, bag inflated.","pH ↓ CO2 ↑ = respiratory acidosis; pH ↓ HCO3 ↓ = metabolic acidosis.","Match the device to the need; COPD SpO2 goal often 88–92%.","Running a nonrebreather below 10 L or with a deflated bag.","The device decides how much oxygen actually reaches the client.","Best device for a precise FiO2 in COPD:",["Venturi mask","Nasal cannula at 6 L","Nonrebreather","Simple face mask"],0),
 C("rc-tpn","Important Nursing Procedures","Tube feeding and TPN","HOB ≥ 30°; verify placement; flush 30 mL. TPN: glucose checks, dedicated line, never stop suddenly.","Coughing during feeds = aspiration. Fever on TPN = line infection.","Stop feeding for aspiration; hang D10W if TPN runs out.","Catching up a behind TPN rate; adding meds to TPN.","Aspiration and glucose swings are the main dangers.","If a TPN bag runs out and the new bag isn't ready, hang:",["10% dextrose","Normal saline","Lactated Ringer's","Nothing until pharmacy arrives"],0),
];
